python eval_agent.py --episodes 10 --seed 42

Those are our ablations 
--------------------------------

1) Main algorithm comparison. {Salma}
Train PPO, PPO-Sparsity, DQN-Dueling, and DQN-Doubling on the exact same traffic patterns, state space, reward function, and action limits. Report mean and standard deviation over at least 5 random seeds for return, latency, cost, dropped requests, and action stability.

2) Sparse-update ablation. {Sherouk}
For each base algorithm, vary the update frequency, such as every step, every 2 steps, every 4 steps, and every 8 steps. This directly tests whether sparse updates improve compute efficiency or hurt control quality, which matches your proposal and the sparse-update idea in the literature.

3) Traffic-stress test. {Salma}
Evaluate on deterministic traffic, Poisson arrivals, and bursty spike traffic. This is important because cloud autoscaling papers often stress that policies must generalize to workload variation, not just one smooth demand pattern.

4) Cold-start test. {Yehya}
Compare performance with and without booting delay. This is one of the best experiments for autoscaling, because proactive policies only matter when the system cannot react instantly.

5) Reward-shaping sensitivity. {Yehya}
Sweep the reward weights 𝛼,𝛽,𝛾,𝜔 and see how much each algorithm depends on the penalty design. This helps show whether one method is inherently more robust or just more sensitive to reward tuning

